{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788115603,"nonce":"7Qtqf/0Z5gGVBsGoHCdVZYY/FQQ=","message":"Open-question resolution 3/3 — default fps caps watched/unwatched (worker, spike 0.1).\n\nUnwatched: 30. Watched: 30 stays right for advance; 60 only for live interactive watching. Both via mangohud MANGOHUD_CONFIGFILE (config/mangohud-agent.conf in the profile) — MangoHud watches the file, so rwa watch (spec 1.4) is one file write to a running game; demonstrated live mid-measurement (free-run ~34fps snapped to a locked 30.0 within seconds, no restart).\n\nThe number matters less than what the spike found around it:\n(1) A hidden Hyprland special workspace gets NO frames at all — the parked bench ran at 1-2 fps = ~4 tps at Normal. runInBackground does not cover frame delivery. run-agent.sh now applies render_unfocused + misc:render_unfocused_fps \u003ccap\u003e + vblank_mode=0 before launch; verified 60fps while hidden. Without those, ANY cap number is fiction (FINDINGS §4b).\n(2) The fps cap and the tick throughput multiply: vanilla Ultrafast needs 60fps for its full 900 tps (mult*2=30 ticks/frame ceiling), while the budgeted advance loop delivers ~1400 tps at 30fps (25ms budget = 75% duty). So the unwatched cap of 30 costs nothing once advance is loop-driven — one more reason 1.3's loop, not vanilla speeds, is the advance mechanism.\n(3) Thermals do not distinguish 30 vs 60 under sustained advance (Tctl plateaus 93.5-94.1C either way, 16.5min curve in FINDINGS §6); the governor keys on duty cycle, not fps. 60fps does hold the plateau even at Normal speed, which is why 30 is the resting default.","files":null}]}